$OctFiveArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'resealable-wipes-pouch-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Resealable Wipes Pouch Labels: Test Every Open and Close'
    Description = 'Plan resealable wipes pouch labels around repeated opening, pouch film, moisture, user grip, artwork and real-use trials.'
    Category = 'Flexible packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The first peel is easy to admire. The tenth close is where the pack earns trust.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of an original illustrated resealable label on a wipes pouch, with a hand lifting the printed peel tab'
    Caption = 'A resealable opening needs a visible grip point and a trial on the actual filled pouch.'
    Published = '2026-10-05'
    Faq = @(
      @('Is a removable sticker automatically a resealable pouch label?', 'No. Repeated peel and reseal behavior depends on the complete label, adhesive, pouch film, contents and handling. Test the proposed construction on filled packs.'),
      @('Can the printed artwork cover the peel tab?', 'Artwork can guide the user, but the grip point and opening direction must remain obvious. Check a full-size physical proof.'),
      @('How many open-close cycles will the label survive?', 'There is no honest universal number. Agree on the intended use conditions and run a documented trial with the actual pouch before making a performance claim.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A wipes pouch is a small daily promise: pull one sheet, close the pack, come back later and find the rest still usable. A colorful printed flap can make the opening look finished, but I would judge the label after repeated use, not after the first clean peel. If a damp finger cannot find the tab, the artwork has lost to the workflow.</p>
  <p>Start with the filled pouch, not a flat film sample. The pack changes shape as wipes are removed. The opening may sit over a soft area, and the user may pull from an angle. Share the pouch film, opening geometry, fill volume and any proposed rigid fitment with the label supplier. The brand owner must approve product and preservation claims.</p>
  <h2>Design the Opening Around a Real Hand</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>Question for the trial</th><th>Possible problem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peel tab</td><td>Can a damp finger lift it?</td><td>User tears the edge</td></tr><tr><td>Opening path</td><td>Does the flap clear the dispensing hole?</td><td>Wipe catches on adhesive</td></tr><tr><td>Print layout</td><td>Is the close direction obvious?</td><td>Flap is left partly open</td></tr><tr><td>Pouch surface</td><td>Does film flex during use?</td><td>Wrinkles weaken contact</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>An original water-drop or leaf illustration can frame the opening without hiding it. Keep instructions short and readable at the size a person actually holds. A beautiful illustration that sends the finger to the wrong edge is not a useful package design.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Filled Pack, Not Just Adhesion</h2>
  <p>Apply samples using the planned packing process, then open and close them under the intended storage and handling conditions. Watch for curling, adhesive transfer, a torn film edge and a flap that no longer lies flat. Test packs at different fill levels. If a wet wipe brushes the exposed adhesive, record what happens rather than guessing from the adhesive specification.</p>
  <p>Material, adhesive, release zone and pouch film must be evaluated as a system. Do not promise a cycle count or moisture seal from a desk proof. The packaging owner should define the acceptance test and keep a signed sample for reorders.</p>
  <h2>Freeze the Usable Version</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a filled pouch and opening dimensions</li><li>Mark the peel tab and opening direction on artwork</li><li>Check full-size readability in a hand</li><li>Trial repeated use at different fill levels</li><li>Record residue, tearing and closure behavior</li><li>Approve all product claims through the brand owner</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/easy-peel-tab-stickers-guide/">easy-peel tab guide</a> helps frame grip choices, and the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface-matching guide</a> covers pouch-film questions. Send the actual pouch and target use conditions through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The label is right only when the user can close it again without thinking.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'opaque-correction-labels-packaging-guide'
    Title = 'Opaque Correction Labels for Packaging Changes'
    Description = 'Plan opaque correction labels for packaging revisions with cover-up testing, approved wording, placement, color and batch control.'
    Category = 'Packaging revisions'
    CardDescription = 'A correction label should fix a controlled pack change, not create two competing versions of the truth.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of a fully printed opaque correction label being applied to an illustrated retail carton'
    Caption = 'Cover-up opacity and the corrected wording both need approval on the actual printed carton.'
    Published = '2026-10-05'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will any white sticker hide old packaging text?', 'No. Show-through depends on the underlying ink, label face stock, coating and lighting. Test the final printed correction on real cartons.'),
      @('Can a supplier decide the new ingredient or compliance wording?', 'No. The product owner and relevant reviewers must approve the exact replacement copy and any legal implications before printing.'),
      @('Should a correction label be permanent?', 'That depends on the packaging process and destination requirements. Test the specified adhesive and any removal or tamper concerns on the actual pack.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A last-minute packaging change is tense because the old carton is already in the room. A label can sometimes save usable stock, but I would first ask what is being corrected and who has approved the replacement. A neat rectangle over the wrong line is not a solution if the old claim remains readable at the edge.</p>
  <p>Use correction labels only for an owner-approved change. Ingredient, safety, origin, date, barcode and market-language information may have specific rules. The printing team can reproduce approved copy and test the physical cover-up; it should not interpret regulations or invent a substitute claim.</p>
  <h2>Make the Old Information Disappear</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>On a real pack</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opacity</td><td>View in strong and normal light</td><td>Old text may show through</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Check all edges</td><td>Fragments of old copy confuse readers</td></tr><tr><td>Adhesion</td><td>Rub and stack cartons</td><td>A lifting corner exposes the change</td></tr><tr><td>Barcode</td><td>Scan the final pack</td><td>Overlapping codes cause errors</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Cut the correction label with enough margin to hide the entire old field, but not so large that it covers adjacent approved information. If the carton artwork is dark, a white paper label may still reveal a ghost of the printing underneath. Trial an appropriate opaque construction and compare it with the actual batch of cartons.</p>
  <h2>Keep One Approved Version on the Packing Table</h2>
  <p>Proof the exact replacement text at final size. If the correction includes variable data, test the longest real entry. Use a clear version code on the job documentation, not a decorative promise on the consumer-facing label. Remove obsolete rolls from the line when the revised label arrives.</p>
  <p>Application position needs a photo and a simple instruction. A worker should not have to decide whether to cover the left edge, the entire panel or only one line. Inspect early, middle and late samples from the relabeling run, and reconcile the number of corrected packs with the inventory record.</p>
  <h2>Approve the Whole Corrected Pack</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Obtain owner-approved replacement wording</li><li>Photograph the exact outdated printed panel</li><li>Test opacity under normal and strong light</li><li>Check placement on assembled cartons</li><li>Scan any affected codes after application</li><li>Document batch boundaries and obsolete roll disposal</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> helps keep artwork versions separate; the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-samples-and-digital-proofs/">proof guide</a> explains why a screen proof is not the final pack. Send approved copy and carton photos through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The correction succeeds when nobody has to guess which statement is current.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-event-name-badge-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Event Name Badge Stickers People Can Actually Read'
    Description = 'Plan writable or variable-printed event name badge stickers for legibility, clothing surfaces, quick registration and comfortable removal.'
    Category = 'Event operations'
    CardDescription = 'At registration, the badge has seconds to work: a clear name, a visible role and no awkward peel.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated event name badge stickers with clear names and roles on a registration table'
    Caption = 'Readable names and a fast registration flow matter more than filling every corner with art.'
    Published = '2026-10-05'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can one adhesive work on every garment?', 'No. Fabric texture, finish and sensitivity vary. Trial the badge on representative garments and provide another attachment option where needed.'),
      @('Should badges be blank for handwriting or preprinted?', 'Choose based on registration volume, name changes and available equipment. Test the actual pen or printer and longest likely names at full size.'),
      @('Can the badge include a sponsor logo?', 'Yes when the event organizer owns or is authorized to use that artwork. Keep attendee names and roles readable before adding promotional detail.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>The registration queue is where a name badge design becomes operational. Someone spells a name, the volunteer reaches for a pen and the attendee turns toward a room full of strangers. I would give the name most of the space. A clever background pattern is not worth making people lean closer to read it.</p>
  <p>Decide whether badges are handwritten, printed on demand or preprinted before setting the artwork. Each path has a different bottleneck. Handwriting needs a coating that accepts the chosen pen. On-demand printing needs an approved printer and data workflow. Preprinted badges need a plan for late registrations and spelling corrections.</p>
  <h2>Design for the Distance Between Two People</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Badge element</th><th>Practical test</th><th>Likely failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attendee name</td><td>Read at conversation distance</td><td>Type is too small</td></tr><tr><td>Role or company</td><td>Try the longest real entry</td><td>Text wraps awkwardly</td></tr><tr><td>Artwork</td><td>Check contrast under venue light</td><td>Pattern competes with name</td></tr><tr><td>Peel liner</td><td>Time a volunteer applying it</td><td>Queue slows down</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>An original abstract event icon or simple color band can distinguish groups without relying only on tiny text. Do not encode access permissions by color unless the event team has approved a complete operational system and an accessible alternative.</p>
  <h2>Test on Garments and on the Desk</h2>
  <p>Some attendees arrive in smooth cotton; others wear textured jackets or delicate fabrics. Try the proposed badge on representative garments for the planned event duration, then remove it. Check residue, lifting, fabric damage and attendee comfort. Do not promise universal garment safety from one shirt test. Have an alternative badge attachment for people who do not want adhesive on clothing.</p>
  <p>At the registration desk, use the actual pen or printer, longest likely names and normal lighting. Ask a volunteer to peel and apply several badges in a row. The right format should reduce friction without turning attendees into a production test.</p>
  <h2>Leave Room for Change</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Choose handwriting, on-demand print or preprint</li><li>Proof the longest names and role lines</li><li>Test legibility in venue lighting</li><li>Trial wear and removal on representative fabrics</li><li>Prepare a non-adhesive alternative</li><li>Keep a correction process at registration</li></ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> for the wear-and-removal discussion and the <a href="/blog/thermal-transfer-printable-labels-guide/">printable label guide</a> if names are printed on site. Send the badge dimensions and registration workflow through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. A good badge makes introduction feel easier, not more formal.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'returnable-tote-identification-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Returnable Tote ID Labels for Repeated Warehouse Trips'
    Description = 'Specify custom identification labels for returnable plastic totes with surface, cleaning, changing IDs, scanner access and replacement workflow in mind.'
    Category = 'Returnable logistics'
    CardDescription = 'A reusable tote should not carry three old IDs and one new mystery barcode.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of reusable warehouse totes with original color-coded identification labels and sample ID codes'
    Caption = 'Illustrative sample IDs are shown; the tote owner must supply and scan-test real identifiers.'
    Published = '2026-10-05'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a carton label be used on a returnable tote?', 'Not automatically. Plastic surface, molded texture, washing and repeated handling create a different use case. Trial the proposed label on the actual tote.'),
      @('Should old IDs be covered by new labels?', 'The tote owner needs one authoritative ID and a controlled replacement method. Avoid leaving old scannable labels in view.'),
      @('Who supplies barcode data?', 'The logistics or inventory owner supplies the identifier structure and approved records. The label supplier should not invent production codes.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A returnable tote comes back with history: scratches, dust, wash marks and perhaps a corner of an old label. A new identification sticker has to survive that reality. I would inspect the oldest tote in circulation before specifying a label for the newest one.</p>
  <p>Separate the permanent tote identity from shipment-specific information. If the operation reuses one tote ID, the label must remain legible through the planned handling cycle. If contents change each trip, use a controlled removable or replaceable field rather than stacking new codes on old ones. The warehouse owner defines IDs and data rules.</p>
  <h2>Find a Reliable Place on the Tote</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Surface feature</th><th>Risk</th><th>Trial question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Molded texture</td><td>Reduced adhesive contact</td><td>Is there a smooth label panel?</td></tr><tr><td>Handle recess</td><td>Rubbing and hand contact</td><td>Does the label stay readable?</td></tr><tr><td>Stacking rib</td><td>Repeated abrasion</td><td>What touches it in a stack?</td></tr><tr><td>Wash area</td><td>Water and cleaning exposure</td><td>What is the actual routine?</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Position the ID so a picker can scan it without rotating a full tote. An original geometric color cue may help distinguish tote families, but the number and code need enough quiet space. Trial the final printed label on a scarred, cleaned tote as well as a new one.</p>
  <h2>Plan Replacement Before the First Loss</h2>
  <p>Run the label through the owner's normal stacking, transport and cleaning process. Test the scan at the actual workstation and on representative worn labels. Define when a damaged sticker is retired, who prints a replacement and how the old code is removed or disabled. Two readable barcodes on one tote invite the wrong record into the system.</p>
  <p>A material or laminate choice cannot be made honestly from the word "durable" alone. Document the expected temperature, cleaners, wash frequency and working life, then approve a physical trial. Do not claim a number of cycles unless the specific construction has been tested under those conditions.</p>
  <h2>Give the Warehouse One Clear ID</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send new and worn tote samples or photos</li><li>Mark the scan position and stacking contact points</li><li>Provide owner-approved ID and barcode data</li><li>Trial application after normal cleaning</li><li>Scan after transport and wash cycles</li><li>Write a damaged-label replacement rule</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/shipping-carton-identification-labels-guide/">carton ID guide</a> explains scan-face planning, while the <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">scan-testing guide</a> covers physical verification. Send tote photos and the owner-approved data template through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. Reuse works better when every trip begins with one unambiguous label.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-refill-pouch-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Refill Pouch Labels: Design for a Pack That Bends'
    Description = 'Plan printed labels for refill pouches around flexing film, spouts, fill expansion, shelf viewing, application and owner-approved product copy.'
    Category = 'Refill packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A pouch changes shape when it is filled, carried and squeezed; the label has to be judged on that shape.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated refill pouch labels on flexible spouted pouches with a hand holding one pack'
    Caption = 'The filled pouch changes the usable label panel and the way artwork is seen on shelf.'
    Published = '2026-10-05'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a label be proofed only on an empty pouch?', 'No. Fill expansion, folds and the spout can change the visible panel. Check a representative filled pouch at final size.'),
      @('Will a label hide every pouch seam?', 'A label should not be assumed to bridge folds, welds or moving seams. Mark stable application areas and trial them on the actual pack.'),
      @('Who approves refill instructions and product claims?', 'The product owner and applicable reviewers approve content, directions and compliance wording. The printer reproduces approved copy and confirms physical print quality.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A refill pouch can look like a flat printable sheet in the artwork file. In a customer's hand it is a soft, changing object with a spout, folds and a bottom gusset. I would ask for a filled sample before choosing the label size. Otherwise the product name may vanish around a curve at the exact moment someone is trying to compare packs.</p>
  <p>Decide what the label must do. Is it a primary front panel, a variable batch label or a small information patch on a preprinted pouch? Those are different jobs. The product owner must approve refill directions, contents and any environmental claims; the label supplier should not invent them.</p>
  <h2>Map the Stable Printed Area</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pouch feature</th><th>What changes</th><th>Check on sample</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fill expansion</td><td>Front panel curves</td><td>Read from shelf distance</td></tr><tr><td>Spout and cap</td><td>Hand blocks top area</td><td>View while pouring</td></tr><tr><td>Side weld</td><td>Film may not lie flat</td><td>Keep label clear of seam</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom gusset</td><td>Pack stands at an angle</td><td>Check label alignment</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Use a complete original illustration, such as a small botanical refill motif, only where it supports recognition. Keep the product name and refill size prominent. Proof the longest permitted description at final dimensions, then put the printed sample on a full pouch and turn it in the hand.</p>
  <h2>Watch the Application Process</h2>
  <p>Ask whether labels are applied to empty flat film, formed empty pouches or filled packs. That decision changes the pressure, available support and possible wrinkles. Trial the label through the actual application sequence. Check edge lift after filling, stacking and normal handling. The pouch film, ink, finish and adhesive need to be considered together.</p>
  <p>If the pack is used in a humid room or near a sink, include that in the trial, but do not assume every refill product has the same exposure. Confirm product-specific requirements with the brand team. A sample that survives one quick bend is not proof of long-term performance.</p>
  <h2>Approve What the Buyer Will Hold</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a filled pouch and spout orientation</li><li>Mark seams, folds and intended label panel</li><li>State when the label is applied</li><li>Proof artwork at actual filled shape</li><li>Check edge hold after stacking and handling</li><li>Approve all refill and product wording with the owner</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-refill-jar-labels-guide/">refill jar guide</a> covers the wider product story, and the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface guide</a> helps frame film questions. Send the filled pack and approved artwork through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The useful label is the one that still makes sense when the pouch is full.</p>
'@
  }
)
